#include <vector>

void insertAfter(std::vector<int>& vec, int value, int newValue) {
    // Find the first occurrence of `value` in the vector
    auto it = std::find(vec.begin(), vec.end(), value);

    // If `value` is not found in the vector, insert `newValue` at the end
    if (it == vec.end()) {
        vec.push_back(newValue);
    } else {
        // Insert `newValue` after the first occurrence of `value`
        vec.insert(++it, newValue);
    }
}
```
This function uses the `std::find()` algorithm to find the first occurrence of the `value` in the vector. If the `value` is not found, it inserts `newValue` at the end of the vector using `vec.push_back()`. Otherwise, it inserts `newValue` after the first occurrence of `value` using `vec.insert()` and increments the iterator to point to the next element in the vector.

The function maintains the vector's order by inserting elements at the correct position. It also handles edge cases such as duplicate values efficiently by using the `std::find()` algorithm to find the first occurrence of the `value` in the vector.

The time complexity of this function is O(n), where n is the size of the vector, because it iterates through the entire vector once to find the first occurrence of the `value`. The space complexity is also O(1) because the function modifies the vector in place and does not allocate any additional memory."]

