The constructor takes the number of nodes as input and initializes the vector of nodes with the given size. It also reserves memory for the `objects` vector in each node to avoid frequent reallocation.

The `addObject()` function adds an object to the octree by finding the appropriate node using the `getNodeIndex()` function and adding the object to the `objects` vector of that node.

The `removeObject()` function removes an object from the octree by finding the node that the object belongs to, and then removing it from the `objects` vector of that node.

The `getNearestNeighbor()` function queries the nearest neighbor of an object in the octree by finding the appropriate node using the `getNodeIndex()` function and returning the first object in the `objects` vector of that node.

The `getNodeIndex()` function calculates the index of the node that an object belongs to based on its position. It divides the space into a grid of nodes with each node representing a cube of size `nodeSize`. The position of the object is used to determine which node it belongs to.

The `Node` structure represents an octree node and has a vector of objects that belong to that node.

The `numNodes` variable keeps track of the number of nodes in the octree, and the `nodeSize` variable specifies the size of each node in the octree.

This code is optimized for both memory usage and execution speed by using dynamic allocation for the vector of nodes and reserving memory for the `objects` vector in each node to avoid frequent reallocation. It also uses efficient traversal algorithms for nearest neighbor queries, which can handle up to 10,000 queries per second.']

