#include <mutex>
#include <unordered_map>

using namespace std;

// Data structure to store the last time an attack was performed for each character
struct LastAttack {
  int characterId;
  chrono::steady_clock::time_point lastAttackTime;
};

// Map to store the last time an attack was performed for each character
unordered_map<int, LastAttack> lastAttacks;

// Mutex to protect concurrent access to the last attacks map
mutex lastAttacksMutex;

void selectAndExecuteAttack(int characterId) {
  // Acquire lock on the last attacks map
  unique_lock<mutex> lock(lastAttacksMutex);

  // Check if an attack has been performed recently for this character
  auto it = lastAttacks.find(characterId);
  if (it != lastAttacks.end()) {
    // If an attack has been performed recently, check if the cooldown period has passed
    chrono::steady_clock::time_point now = chrono::steady_clock::now();
    if (now - it->second.lastAttackTime > chrono::seconds(9)) {
      // If the cooldown period has passed, select a new attack and update the last attacks map
      int attackNumber = random() % 2 + 1;
      LastAttack newLastAttack{characterId, now};
      it->second = newLastAttack;
      cout << "Character " << characterId << " selected attack number " << attackNumber << endl;
    } else {
      // If the cooldown period has not passed, do nothing and return
      return;
    }
  } else {
    // If no attack has been performed recently for this character, select a new attack and update the last attacks map
    int attackNumber = random() % 2 + 1;
    LastAttack newLastAttack{characterId, chrono::steady_clock::now()};
    lastAttacks[characterId] = newLastAttack;
    cout << "Character " << characterId << " selected attack number " << attackNumber << endl;
  }
}
```
This implementation uses an `unordered_map` to store the last time an attack was performed for each character. The map is protected by a mutex to ensure thread safety. The function `selectAndExecuteAttack()` acquires a lock on the map before checking if an attack has been performed recently for the given character. If an attack has been performed recently, the function checks if the cooldown period has passed using the `chrono` library. If the cooldown period has not passed, the function does nothing and returns. If no attack has been performed recently or the cooldown period has passed, the function selects a new attack number randomly and updates the last attacks map.

The performance implications of this design are:

* The `unordered_map` is a fast data structure for storing key-value pairs, making it suitable for concurrent access.
* The mutex protects the map from concurrent access, ensuring that only one thread can modify the map at a time. This reduces contention and improves performance.
* The use of `chrono` library for timing operations is efficient and provides accurate results.

Overall, this design is efficient and scalable, allowing multiple characters to perform attacks concurrently while ensuring compliance with game mechanics constraints.']

