const asyncReduce = (arr, callback) => {
  // Initialize variables for tracking progress and potential errors
  let currentIndex = 0;
  let totalElements = arr.length;
  let partialSum = 0;
  let error = null;

  // Define a function to handle the reduction operation asynchronously
  const reduceAsync = async () => {
    while (currentIndex < totalElements) {
      try {
        // Retrieve the next element from the array
        const element = arr[currentIndex];

        // Perform the reduction operation on the current element
        partialSum += await callback(element);

        // Increment the index for the next iteration
        currentIndex++;
      } catch (err) {
        // If an error occurs, store it and retry after a delay
        error = err;
        setTimeout(() => reduceAsync(), 1000 * Math.pow(2, currentIndex));
        break;
      }
    }

    if (!error) {
      // Return the final partial sum if no errors occurred
      return partialSum;
    } else {
      // If an error occurred, return a specific error object
      return new Error("External service unreachable");
    }
  };

  // Start the reduction operation asynchronously
  reduceAsync();
};
```
This function takes an array of numbers and a callback function that performs the reduction operation on each element. It uses asynchronous programming to handle large datasets efficiently and manage potential errors gracefully. The function also includes a retry mechanism with exponential backoff for handling intermittent failures from the external service.

To test the function, you can use a large sample array of numbers and verify its performance and error handling under various conditions. For example:
```javascript
