    for (i = 0; i < num_tasks; i++) {
        free(tasks[i].data);
    }
}

// Handler functions for different types of operations
void sort_handler(void* data) {
    // Sort the data buffer using a specific algorithm
    qsort(data, sizeof(task_t), compare_func);
}

void search_handler(void* data) {
    // Search the data buffer for a specific value
    int* value = (int*)malloc(sizeof(int));
    *value = 5;
    int index = binary_search(data, value, sizeof(task_t), compare_func);
    if (index >= 0) {
        printf("Found value %d at index %d\n", *value, index);
    } else {
        printf("Value not found\n");
    }
}

void transform_handler(void* data) {
    // Transform the data buffer using a specific algorithm
    int* new_data = (int*)malloc(sizeof(int));
    *new_data = 10;
    memcpy(data, new_data, sizeof(task_t));
}

// Utility functions for sorting and searching
int compare_func(const void* a, const void* b) {
    return (*(int*)a - *(int*)b);
}

int binary_search(void* data, int* value, size_t size, int (*compare)(const void*, const void*)) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = size - 1;
    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = (low + high) / 2;
        int cmp = compare(value, (int*)data + mid);
        if (cmp < 0) {
            high = mid - 1;
        } else if (cmp > 0) {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            return mid;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```
This code defines a structure `task_t` to represent a task, which contains pointers to a data buffer and a handler function pointer. The `manage_tasks()` function allocates memory for multiple tasks using `malloc()`, initializes them with different handlers and data buffers, and frees the allocated memory when the function completes using `free()`.

The `sort_handler()`, `search_handler()`, and `transform_handler()` functions are defined as handler functions for sorting, searching, and transforming the data buffer respectively. These functions use utility functions such as `qsort()`, `binary_search()`, and `memcpy()` to perform their respective operations.

The code also includes edge cases such as varying data sizes and handler complexities to enhance the robustness of the implementation.']

