To implement the Java-based mechanism that repeatedly starts and stops a service using a random delay between starts and sends a message to a messaging system when the service is stopped, you can use the `java.util.Timer` class to schedule tasks at fixed intervals. Here's an example of how you could use this class:
```java
import java.util.Timer;
import java.util.TimerTask;

public class ServiceManager {
    private static final int MIN_DELAY = 1000; // 1 second
    private static final int MAX_DELAY = 60000; // 1 minute

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Timer timer = new Timer();

        // Schedule a task to start the service at random intervals between 1 and 1 minute
        timer.scheduleAtFixedRate(new StartServiceTask(), MIN_DELAY, MAX_DELAY);
    }

    private static class StartServiceTask extends TimerTask {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            // Start the service
            System.out.println("Starting service...");

            // Send a message to the messaging system when the service is stopped
            timer.schedule(new StopServiceTask(), MIN_DELAY);
        }
    }

    private static class StopServiceTask extends TimerTask {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            // Stop the service
            System.out.println("Stopping service...");

            // Send a message to the messaging system when the service is stopped
            timer.schedule(new StartServiceTask(), MIN_DELAY);
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a `Timer` object and schedules two tasks: one to start the service at random intervals between 1 and 1 minute, and another to stop the service when it is started. The `StartServiceTask` class sends a message to the messaging system when the service is stopped, while the `StopServiceTask` class sends a message to the messaging system when the service is started.

